✅ Runnable → same task, multiple threads (WORKS)
class MyTask implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            "Running by: " + Thread.currentThread().getName()
        );
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Runnable task = new MyTask();

        new Thread(task, "Thread-1").start();
        new Thread(task, "Thread-2").start();
        new Thread(task, "Thread-3").start();
    }
}

✔ Output (order may vary)
Running by: Thread-1
Running by: Thread-2
Running by: Thread-3


🔍 What this proves

Same Runnable object (task)

Multiple Thread objects

Each thread executes the same task

✔ Works perfectly

❌ Thread → single execution only (FAILS)
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running...");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MyThread t = new MyThread();

        t.start();   // ✔ works
        t.start();   // ❌ IllegalThreadStateException
    }
}


🔍 What this proves

Same Thread object

start() called twice

❌ Runtime exception

🔑 One-line conclusion (matches your sentence)

Runnable represents a reusable task that can be executed by
 multiple Thread objects, whereas a Thread represents a
 single execution that can be started only once.